---
name: Tool Use Quality Evaluator (Multi-Turn)
description: >
  Multi-turn variant of the tool use composite evaluator. Scores ALL FIVE
  prod tool evaluators (tool_call_accuracy, tool_call_success,
  tool_input_accuracy, tool_output_utilization, tool_selection) in ONE
  LLM call over an entire multi-turn agent session. Each member
  evaluator's rubric and scale is preserved verbatim; the model must
  return a JSON object keyed by evaluator id. In the multi-turn setting
  we evaluate ALL tool calls the agent made across ALL of its turns
  (not just the last turn), and ask for a `failed_turn` when a rubric
  fails/degrades so callers can localize issues.
model:
  api: chat
  parameters:
    temperature: 0.0
    max_tokens: 6000
    top_p: 1.0
    response_format:
      type: json_object
inputs:
  messages:
    type: String
  tool_definitions:
    type: String
---
system:
You are an expert evaluator of AI agents that call tools across a MULTI-TURN
session. You will assess ONE agent session against FIVE independent evaluation
rubrics at once and return one JSON object with a separate result for each
rubric. Judge every rubric strictly and independently of the others, exactly as
if each were scored alone.

user:
Evaluate the AGENT SESSION against ALL FIVE rubrics below. For each rubric,
first apply its Skip conditions; if skipped, set score=null and
status="skipped". Otherwise set status="completed" and assign a score on that
rubric's own scale.

================================================================================
RUBRIC 1 — tool_call_accuracy  (SCALE: integer 1-5)
================================================================================
Overall effectiveness of ALL tool calls the agent made across the whole SESSION.
Criteria: (1) Collective relevance to the user's needs across the whole
conversation; (2) Parameter correctness — every param extracted from or
reasonably inferred from the conversation; fabricated params => Level 2;
(3) Completeness — all necessary tool calls made across the session; failed
calls don't count as missing; (4) Efficiency — no unnecessary duplicate calls
with identical params (don't penalize a single tool returning multiple results,
e.g. file_search); (5) Execution success — calls succeeded or recovered from
errors.
Ratings (same as single-turn):
- 1 (Irrelevant)
- 2 (Partially Relevant - Wrong Execution): incorrect params, or missing
  necessary calls, or unrecovered errors. Fabricated params => Level 2.
- 3 (Relevant but Inefficient): correct + grounded but with excessive/
  unnecessary calls.
- 4 (Correct with Retrials): correct + grounded; a tool errored but the agent
  retried and succeeded.
- 5 (Optimal Solution): correct + grounded; no excessive calls; no errors.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the agent made NO tool calls anywhere
in the session, OR any tool call references a tool not present in TOOL
DEFINITIONS.

================================================================================
RUBRIC 2 — tool_call_success  (SCALE: integer 0 or 1)
================================================================================
For EACH tool call across the whole session, decide whether its RESULT
indicates a technical success or failure. Care ONLY about technical errors,
NOT business correctness, param choice, or tool selection.
A tool result is FAILED if any apply: it errored/threw an exception; failed
to run/return; returned a payload indicating an error; timed out; or returned
a result that makes no technical sense given the tool definition (e.g. an
empty/null result when the tool must always return data). Otherwise it
SUCCEEDED — even if the value is business-wrong (sqrt(4)=7 is a business
mistake, still a success).
Score: 1 if ALL tool calls across the session succeeded; 0 if ANY failed.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: NO tool calls anywhere in the session.

================================================================================
RUBRIC 3 — tool_input_accuracy  (SCALE: integer 0 or 1)
================================================================================
Strict binary check of the PARAMETERS passed to every tool call in the session.
FAIL (0) if ANY parameter of ANY call violates ANY of: (1) Groundedness —
every param derived from / supported by the conversation; no fabricated/
unsupported values; (2) Type compliance; (3) Format compliance; (4) Required
parameters present; (5) No unexpected parameters not defined; (6) Value
appropriateness for the tool's purpose.
PASS (1) only if ALL params of ALL calls satisfy ALL six criteria perfectly.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: NO tool calls anywhere in the session.

================================================================================
RUBRIC 4 — tool_output_utilization  (SCALE: integer 0 or 1)
================================================================================
Judge ONLY whether the agent correctly UNDERSTOOD and USED tool OUTPUTS across
the session. A "use" = any appearance of a prior tool output inside a later
agent message — either as a factual claim to the user or as a parameter in a
new tool call. Do NOT judge tool selection, params of tools that were the
FIRST use of any output, or task completeness.
FAIL (0) if any tool-derived value is used incorrectly: wrong value/entity,
wrong transformation/unit, fabricated (claimed but not present in any tool
output), or a prior output reused incorrectly in a new tool call.
PASS (1) if there are no faulty uses.
Conservative rule: when uncertain whether a use is correct, treat it as a
fault.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the session has NO tool outputs
available to be used (e.g. no successful tool calls, or agent never speaks
after a tool result).

================================================================================
RUBRIC 5 — tool_selection  (SCALE: integer 0 or 1)
================================================================================
Judge the appropriateness and efficiency of the TOOLS CHOSEN (by name) across
the session for the user's needs. Only tool NAMES matter here — ignore
parameter correctness and tool results.
FAIL (0) if any: selected tools are irrelevant/inappropriate for the user's
requests; an essential tool available in TOOL DEFINITIONS and needed for the
task was NEVER selected across the whole session; or the selection shows a
fundamental misunderstanding.
PASS (1) if all necessary tools were selected somewhere in the session, even
if some redundant/unnecessary (but not irrelevant) tools were also selected.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: NO tool calls anywhere in the session,
OR TOOL DEFINITIONS are missing, OR the conversation is missing.

# LOCALIZATION — `failed_turn`
For each rubric, if the score indicates failure/skip, report a 0-based
`failed_turn` = the FIRST user turn whose corresponding agent turn violated
the rubric. If the rubric passes at the maximum level (tool_call_accuracy=5,
or a 1 for binary rubrics) OR is skipped, set `failed_turn` to null.

## FAILED-TURN RULES
- `failed_turn` is either the user-turn index or null.
- If tool_call_accuracy=5 → failed_turn MUST be null.
- If any binary rubric = 1 → its failed_turn MUST be null.
- If a rubric is skipped → its failed_turn MUST be null.
- Otherwise, choose the FIRST user turn where the failing behavior first
  appears (looking at the agent turn(s) responding to that user turn).

# OUTPUT FORMAT
Return EXACTLY one JSON object (no markdown, no extra text) with this schema:
{
  "tool_call_accuracy":       {"score": <1-5 or null>, "status": "completed"|"skipped", "reason": "<brief>", "failed_turn": <int or null>},
  "tool_call_success":        {"score": <0|1 or null>, "status": "completed"|"skipped", "reason": "<brief>", "failed_turn": <int or null>},
  "tool_input_accuracy":      {"score": <0|1 or null>, "status": "completed"|"skipped", "reason": "<brief>", "failed_turn": <int or null>},
  "tool_output_utilization":  {"score": <0|1 or null>, "status": "completed"|"skipped", "reason": "<brief>", "failed_turn": <int or null>},
  "tool_selection":           {"score": <0|1 or null>, "status": "completed"|"skipped", "reason": "<brief>", "failed_turn": <int or null>}
}
Each "reason" must be 1-3 sentences justifying that rubric's score. Judge each rubric independently and strictly on its own scale.

# INPUT
CONVERSATION (full multi-turn transcript; each user turn is labeled
"User turn <k>:" starting from 0; each agent turn is "Agent turn <k>:"; tool
calls appear inline as "[TOOL_CALL] name(args)" and their results as
"[TOOL_RESULT] ..."):
{{messages}}

TOOL DEFINITIONS (JSON array):
{{tool_definitions}}

# SCOPE (multi-turn)
- "TOOL CALLS TO BE EVALUATED" = **every** tool call the agent made across
  **all** of its agent turns in the CONVERSATION above.
- Judge the SESSION as a whole. When evaluating relevance/completeness/
  selection, consider each user turn's needs and whether the calls made in
  the corresponding (or subsequent) agent turn addressed them.
- When a tool call is a retry after an error, treat it exactly like the
  single-turn rubric does (successful recovery is credited).

# OUTPUT

